## 7. Batch Execution Rule

Only perform work inside the explicitly requested batch scope.

Do not infer or expand the scope.

Do not perform broad repository-wide replacements unless the batch explicitly says to do so.

Do not rename, move, or delete files unless the batch explicitly authorizes it.

Prefer this batch pattern:

1. planning report
2. manifest matrix
3. reviewed execution
4. integrity scan
5. closeout report

## 8. Absolute Safety Rules

Do not use PowerShell `Set-Content`.

Do not normalize encoding.

Do not run formatters.

Do not rewrite Korean body text.

Do not rewrite prose unless explicitly requested.

Do not modify SQL, migrations, application code, Supabase functions, packages, or runtime code during documentation governance batches.

Do not create runtime implementation.

Do not delete files unless explicitly instructed.

Do not create new files under old five-digit paths.

Do not create new five-digit Markdown files.

## 9. Encoding Rule

Preserve UTF-8.

Preserve Korean text.

Avoid tools or commands that may corrupt Korean encoding.

When editing existing Markdown files, use targeted safe edits only.

## 10. Link and H1 Rule

When a file basename is renamed:

- Update H1 only if H1 exactly mirrors the old filename.
- Do not force-edit H1 if it is a deliberate title.
- Internal links should be updated in a separate integrity batch unless the current batch explicitly includes link updates.
- Historical migration records may keep old paths as audit evidence if clearly historical.

## 11. Long Path Rule

This project has long path risk.

Keep folder names short where possible.

Prefer short stable folder names such as:

```text
600000_implementation_lifecycle
605000_pos_gateway_package
700000_runtime_flow
```

Do not create long explanatory folder names unless explicitly approved.

